<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  `F-158`, rewritten by `F-185` — the bubble's ground.

  ## It is an oval now, and that is the fix

  It used to be a rectangle with a 28 dp corner radius, which is a true circle
  **only while the view is square**. That was deliberate: the bubble could
  stretch into a pill to say something, and a rectangle-with-radius becomes a
  correct pill on its own, where an oval would become a lozenge with curved
  sides.

  The owner reported the consequence: *"its also becoming square… it should be
  circle only not become rounded rectangle"*. Tapping the bubble made the pill
  appear — with the word *Scanning…* in it, before anything was scanning — and
  a pill is what a rounded rectangle looks like when you were expecting a
  circle.

  `F-185` deleted the pill. A tap opens a fan of two discs instead, so the
  bubble is never anything but square, and an oval is now the honest shape:
  **round by construction rather than by keeping two numbers in agreement.**
  The 28 dp that used to have to track `buildBubble`'s 56 dp is gone, and so
  is the gate rule that watched it.

  ## Why the colours are hard-coded rather than taken from the theme

  Same rule as the camera overlays in `swip_tokens.dart`: this is drawn over an
  arbitrary screen that SWIP does not control and cannot measure. It has to
  carry its own contrast regardless of the app underneath, the wallpaper, or
  whether the user is in Paper or Foil. A bubble that followed the app theme
  would be a white circle on a white screen exactly when somebody needed to
  find it.

  ## Why it is gold now, and why the inner disc is gone — `F-189`

  It used to be an ink circle with a gold disc inside it, so the mark sat in a
  9 dp ink ring. The owner asked for *"less black bordered and more yellow"*,
  and the right answer was not to thin the ring but to delete it: gold field,
  ink mark is what `ic_launcher_foreground` already is. The floating button now
  looks like SWIP's app icon instead of its negative, which is one fewer thing
  for a user to learn.

  That took `swip_bubble_disc.xml` with it — a gold disc on a gold ground is
  not a disc — and the glyph is drawn straight onto this shape.
-->
<shape xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:shape="oval">

    <solid android:color="@color/swip_bubble_ground" />
    <stroke
        android:width="1dp"
        android:color="@color/swip_bubble_edge" />
</shape>
